A||N||13||warkior||Fiero Plugs||||6||
Z||000000||warkior||03-21-2001||05:09 AM||||Hey, I'm considering getting a Fiero.  86 SE 2m6.  Any thoughts or suggestions that might help?  It appears to be in almost mint condition.  190000 km's, new clutch, well kept.<P>I heard from one individual that to change the plugs one must drop the engine.  Is that true?||||reg||6
Z||000001||onfire||03-21-2001||05:14 AM||||No.<BR>It can be done without dropping the engine. Although it may be easier to drop the engine. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||03-21-2001||06:29 AM||||Taking off the deck lid makes it easy. Always put anti sieze on the treads and you will have no furthur problems.<BR> Just got through changing a set that had so much rust between the nut face and cylinder head I had to use a mirror flashlight and dental tool and air to clean it out before a socket would fit. They were a different brand than the rear facing plugs and probably original.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||03-21-2001||08:00 AM||||It's not too bad changing the v-6 plugs without taking the lid off or dropping the engine, at least on the nitchback body style.  I imagine the GT body is a little more difficult.<P>Use a short extension on a ratchet for the front plugs.  Antisieze on the threads is a great idea.||||reg||1
Z||000004||stsmithgt||03-21-2001||12:05 PM||||Having one leg in the trunk helped me get to the ones in the front.  Patients lowers the chance of getting cut on something too. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||03-21-2001||12:53 PM||||I have an 86 SE V-6 also. Changing the plugs without removing the decklid or dropping the engine is easy. Just use a flex-head ratchet and a short extension. 15 minutes and all 6 plugs are changed.||||reg||1
Z||000006||warkior||03-21-2001||01:03 PM||||Awesome.  Thanks.  Maybe getting it today.  Thanks for the advice.||||reg||14
Z||000007||Veronica's V6 GT||03-21-2001||10:34 PM||||Come on guys.  You don't have to drop the engine. You don't have to take off the trunk lid.  Just take everything out of your and stand in it. Now you can easily reach the plugs on the fire wall side and what you can't see you can easily feel your way. It should only take you about 15 minutes to do the back plugs. Also it is a good idea to use the antiseize compound  Been there done that.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Veronica's V6 GT||03-21-2001||10:37 PM||||Oops double post. Computer network problems.<p>[This message has been edited by Veronica's V6 GT (edited 03-21-2001).]||||reg||1
